  "details": "The problem of select users being annoying and it being difficult to specifically track down who's doing what is what I wish to address in this thread.\n\nI don't like just flat out disabling features on my security settings. Trusted users aren't called \"trusted users\" because they're going to piss around and misuse their perks; I shouldn't have to void this trust because of distaste to how a select few people use them. Needless to say, I don't like having to use such an extreme solution, because it's unfair to everyone else and there are certain avatars that employ these features that I really like and want to see.\n\nMore often than not, I find myself having to block/unblock avatars with trial and error to find out who's using the feature I dislike. I believe that adding smaller indicators on top of nameplate feature icons may be a potential remedy to this issue. For instance: The icon of a shader could have a green or yellow icon appended to it (since red usually means \"completely disabled\" in the eyes of most) that basically says \"This avatar does/does not employ use of this feature\". This indicator would only show up for permissions that you can already see on an avatar, so if a new user is using an avatar with sounds and you're under default safety settings, there will be no indicator on sounds since it's not even active in the first place.\n\nWhile this does not make it so that I can instantly flag down who's doing what in all cases, it will drastically reduce the time I spend on trial and error so that I may track down who's being annoying faster.\n\nThe second part to this feature that closely couples in with this \"trial and error\" method is the ability to sort players by proximity. Generally speaking, I won't suspect the annoying user is somewhere across the map - I'll look into users closer to me first. The primary rationale for suggesting this is that it can sometimes be difficult to locate the pill collider that all avatars employ so that I can actually click on and block the user's avatar. If I can view players by proximity, I can go through enabling and disabling avatars in an order that feels more in sync to who I suspect is being problematic. One other method of showing this might be to enable holding RMB while the menu is open to draw an outline that always renders on top around all pill colliders so that I know where to click. This would likely be far simpler to implement.\n\nAny immediate opinions on the proposed ideas?",
